 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Schematic thinking: This mode of thinking involves finding the right piece configuration for any given position, and the specific moves or maneuvers to reach that configuration. (2) Paralysis: This is a concept previously covered, but one that is especially helpful to keep in mind in endgames. The limited amount of pieces on the board makes it more likely that one side’s entire army is left paralyzed. Besides this, the limited material count also means that even if only one piece is paralysed, it can have greater consequences than in the opening or middlegame. (3) Fortresses: A fortress is a powerful defensive tool. Finding the right piece configuration can help the defending side to make a draw even when they have a passive position or a material deficit. (4) Pawns in the endgame: An understanding of the topics covered in the ‘Pawn Play’ chapter will be helpful when navigating pawns in the endgame. In this chapter, our focus was on the relationship between the pawns and the minor pieces. In particular, you should be very careful not to arrange your pawns on the same color as your bishop as this can often swing the evaluation of a game from equal to much worse or even losing! (5) The role of the king: The king has been largely left out of the course, since most of the time the king is kept purposefully passive. However, in the endgame things change dramatically, and the power of each side’s king can be the decisive factor as the battle draws to a close. In this section we focused on the connection between the quality of both side’s pawns and the activity of the king, while also highlighting the king’s versatility as both an attacking and defending piece. } 1. { -KEY- } -- 
